KEY BUGS IDENTIFIED:

BUG-1 [CRITICAL — FALSE POSITIVES]: Typosquatting detector is badly calibrated.
  Legitimate well-known packages flagged as CRITICAL typosquats:
    "chalk" flagged as looking like "chai" (used in axios, colinhacks/zod, shelljs, google/zx)
    "jest" flagged as looking like "next" (tj/commander.js)
    "vite" flagged as looking like "vue" (colinhacks/zod)
    "globby" flagged as looking like "glob" (colinhacks/zod, google/zx, sindresorhus/del)
    "xo" flagged as looking like "pg" (sindresorhus/got, node-fetch, chalk, sindresorhus/del)
    "c8" flagged as looking like "pg" (sindresorhus/got, node-fetch, chalk, google/zx)
    "d3" flagged as looking like "pg" (gchq/CyberChef)
    "colors" flagged as looking like "cors" (gchq/CyberChef)
    "cbor" flagged as looking like "cors" (gchq/CyberChef)
    "oxlint" flagged as looking like "eslint" (isaacs/node-glob)
    "cypress" flagged as looking like "express" (OWASP/NodeGoat)
    "np" flagged as looking like "pg" (sindresorhus/got)
    "pem" flagged as looking like "pg" (sindresorhus/got)
    "slash" flagged as looking like "sass" (sindresorhus/del)
    "matcha" flagged as looking like "mocha" (chalk)
    "jsr" flagged as looking like "jest" (colinhacks/zod, google/zx)
    "jshint" flagged as looking like "eslint" (OWASP/NodeGoat)
  Root cause: Levenshtein threshold too loose AND reference package list includes wrong pairings.
  Fix needed: raise distance threshold, whitelist popular packages, or use download-based verification.

BUG-2 [HIGH — FALSE POSITIVE]: "Dynamically constructed URL" fires massively on test suites.
  node-fetch: 162 CRITICAL findings — all in test fixtures where URLs built for testing HTTP library.
  axios: 1 CRITICAL for same reason. This is expected behavior for any HTTP library.
  Fix needed: skip test/ and __tests__/ directories, or require URL to be fetched (not just built).

BUG-3 [HIGH — FALSE POSITIVE]: "Long base64 string" fires on commit SHAs and encoded data.
  execa (5 HIGH), octokit (3 HIGH), chalk (2 HIGH), got (2 HIGH) — these are commit SHAs or
  normal base64 in test snapshots, not obfuscation. The pattern is too broad.
  Fix needed: require minimum length (e.g. 100+ chars) and exclude files in test/ or *.md.

BUG-4 [HIGH — SCANNER SCANS ITS OWN DOCS]: truststar-app/truststar scores 51/SUSPICIOUS.
  CLAUDE.md contains security path examples (e.g. ~/.ssh, /etc/passwd as documentation).
  The scanner reads .md files and flags these as CRITICAL filesystem accesses.
  Fix needed: exclude *.md files and CLAUDE.md from the code scanner.
  Additionally: the "eval(atob())" CRITICAL findings in truststar codebase are real and need review.

BUG-5 [MEDIUM — THRESHOLD]: juice-shop/juice-shop scores 70/SAFE.
  Deliberately vulnerable app (OWASP) should be SUSPICIOUS at minimum. Score at exact threshold.
  Note: scoring formula says SAFE >= 70, so 70 = SAFE is correct by spec but problematic for UX.

BUG-6 [LOW — SEVERITY]: example.com in example/ directories flagged as HIGH.
  Express examples use "example.com" as placeholder per RFC 2606 — this is standard practice.
  Fix needed: downgrade example.com, example.org, example.net to INFO.

BUG-7 [LOW — DEDUP]: "Hardcoded URL to unknown domain" not deduplicated.
  Multiple findings with same domain in same or different files fire separately instead of
  being merged as "found N times". This inflates HIGH counts significantly.

